WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.540
Thank you to curiosity stream for sponsoring this video.

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:08.700
When I came up with the idea for this video I thought oh, maybe I'll pop down to Office Works,

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:15.160
which is the big stationery shop here in Australia, and I'll buy myself a slide rule to do this video with.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.540
But luckily just before I left, I thought you know what maybe,

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.920
just maybe I should check the catalogue first to see if they sell

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.580
slide rules at office works and turns out that they don't.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:31.760
So that's how I've ended up here today with a very makeshift slide rule

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.400
and this is just some pieces of paper held together with a piece of plastic

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.280
that I actually cut out of something I scrounged out of the recycling, this old croissant packet.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.560
And it's held together with a little bit of Blu Tack at the back there.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:52.620
I'll put a link to where you can download the template to make one of these yourself if you so desire

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:57.200
but as I'm going to go through and solve some old exam problems with this

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.140
you'll probably see that it's not quite recommended.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.940
I've become somewhat of an expert in the last half an hour or so on slide rules.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.580
Before that I knew nothing about them and I'd never used one before.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:16.220
Now I have mild competence at using one to do things but it's been a bit of a nightmare actually

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:20.400
so if you haven't used one either, I'd probably say you're not missing out on too much

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:26.460
but there's probably a lot of people maybe even watching this video and around that actually remember slide rules

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:33.220
in their hey-day and they were popular and what was used to do multiplication and division

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.820
as well as things like trigonometry and logs

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:40.980
square roots things like that before our pocket calculators.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:46.780
So I think even in like the 50s and even up to the 70s you would see engineers

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:50.760
with one of these to do all their calculations with.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.900
Apparently it's the slide rule - and doing arithmetic with it - that got us to space.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:01.400
You know, they were used by engineers during like the NASA programs and things like that.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.640
So I guess we owe a lot to the humble slide rule,

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.000
and maybe it's a shame that I've never picked one up before and had to play with it.

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.260
In a previous video of mine, I looked at an MIT entrance exam from

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:19.340
1869 and I looked at the algebra section. If you've seen that video then know that this

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:23.620
exam I have here and the questions I'm going to go through with my slide rule

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:27.680
are from the arithmetic section of that same exam.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.900
So MIT, 1869.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:37.320
It was actually on my previous video about some 19th century arithmetic that some people in the comments

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:43.120
pointed out to me the slide rule and reminded me of something, the log table, as well,

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:48.720
which I didn't actually mention at the time but was probably offered to students back then taking exams

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:53.960
and might have made things easier for them since they didn't have the pocket calculator

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.260
that we might have in an exam today.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:01.740
So let's take a closer look at our slide rule here. Most of it we're not going to use today;

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.380
we're just going to focus on lines C and D.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:10.240
C is the one that slides and D is the one here that it meets.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:16.540
So you can see that C and D are both on a log scale because the distance between

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:22.340
say 1 and 2 is much greater than the distances up here later between like 8 &amp; 9.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:28.360
This is because this part of the slide rule which is used for multiplication and division

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.840
is exploiting properties of logs and specifically the property that

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:37.880
log(x) + log(y) = log(x*y).

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:43.920
So we want to find the product x*y, what we'd do is we can add the log of x to the log of y

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.340
which we kind of do by sliding it like this

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:50.700
and then take the antilog of what we get, the product,

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:55.589
and this scale is sort of set up so that it's taking the antilog for you

00:03:55.589 --> 00:03:58.680
and all you need to do is worry about sliding it around.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:04.320
I think these scales up here (A) is to do with squares and square roots,

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:07.740
and K is to do with cubes and cube roots.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.280
So let's have a look at our exam now.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:13.400
This is it here, these are our MIT arithmetic questions.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:15.980
If we're looking at question 1:

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:22.240
Find the sum, then the difference, and then the product of the fractions

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:26.800
(3 and 5/9) and (1 and 7/24)

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:30.440
and then divide that first fraction by the second one

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:34.720
So I'm actually going to skip finding the sum and the difference

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:38.960
because I don't think it's worth trying to use this slide rule for that and

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:43.240
instead we'll just look at finding the product of these two fractions.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:49.380
Now first we want to convert them from being mixed into being like improper fractions.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:56.920
So let's do 3 times 9 which is 27 plus 5 (ninths)

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.540
= 32 / 9.

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:03.840
That's going to be multiplied by 24 plus 7

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:07.340
= 31 / 24

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:13.700
So our first difficult multiplication we need to do is 32 times 31.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:16.620
Let's do it on our slide rule.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:25.240
So we're going to take the 1 on the C scale and slide it along to the first number that we're multiplying.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:32.960
All the numbers need to be less than 10, so we're actually going to slide this one along to 3.2

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:37.040
and then deal with fixing our decimal places later.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:41.000
So I've got my 1 on 3.2 there.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:45.080
And then we're going to take this plastic slider

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.940
and slide it across to the second number that we're multiplying which is 31.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:52.600
Make sure everyone stays in place.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:56.920
31 will be 3.1 so that'll be...

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:01.760
Right in here. Actually it's extremely close to the end

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:07.240
and I'm going to say - that's extremely close to the end of the scale -

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:12.740
I'm going to say that's 9.95 down here on D.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:17.660
So I'll put 995.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:22.060
I'm not writing 9 point 95 because I'm aware that

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:24.780
what I'm multiplying would give me a number much bigger than that

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:27.700
so I'm pretty sure it's going to be that one there (995).

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:32.440
And then let's do the bottom multiplication. We're going to do 9 by 24.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:38.500
Now this time if we were to try and take this 1 and slide it over to 9,

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:42.600
then the rest of our slide was going to be over here and that's of no use to us.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:46.760
So we're actually going to use the other side, the 1 on the right,

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:51.840
and slide it back over to 9.

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:53.850
Try and get it good...

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:57.000
Okay, and then we're going to take our plastic bit

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:03.180
and slide it over to 2.4 on the C scale.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:07.040
So 2.4 would be about there.

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:11.140
That corresponds on the D scale to be

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:16.560
2.15 I would say. So just in there.

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:22.800
I don't think it's 2.15 because that wouldn't make sense with these numbers

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:26.360
instead it makes sense for it to be 215.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:30.660
So that's actually our fraction that we get as an answer.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:34.240
I'm kind of confident about these first two significant figures,

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:37.600
but the third significant figure I'll be honest was kind of a guess.

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:41.640
So maybe not the most accurate and I feel like most of that's got to do with

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:47.800
The precision here so it's like flimsy pieces of paper and this is barely holding together sensibly

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:53.360
But maybe if you had a more robust slide rule you'd be more confident in your last figure there

00:07:53.560 --> 00:08:01.240
So I actually just looked up on my calculator what this would be and it looks like it would be

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:07.720
992 / 216

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:10.540
So we were actually pretty close there.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:14.320
If we wanted to get some more precision than our slide rule

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:19.020
we could use a log table, which is this crazy table over here,

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:22.860
and we're going to use that in the next question to get a little bit more accuracy.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:26.720
But I think our sort of approximation here was kind of all right.

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:32.480
I'm going to skip dividing this fraction by this one because it's almost the same as what we've just done.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:36.480
Instead of dividing I would suggest multiplying

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:43.680
by this fractions reciprocal so that would make a similar calculation.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:47.660
So I don't think you need to see that again instead we'll do number 2:

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:54.520
Multiply 73 thousandths, which would be 73 / 1000

00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:01.480
by 19 hundreds which would be this (19 / 100).

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:03.310
Okay

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:05.760
So if we want to do that

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:09.860
we should exploit the property of logs that the

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:18.260
log(73) + log(19)

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:25.120
will give us the log of the product, 73 * 19.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:29.520
So if we wanted to find this product here 73 times 19,

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:35.100
then all we need to do is add the logs together and then take the antilog of the answer.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:42.620
So let's try and do that. We're going to look up in our log table what the log of 73 is

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:45.280
Now in a similar way,

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:51.660
these numbers are all less than 10. So let's have a look for 7.3,

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:57.640
and here it is here. It's a little small, but it corresponds to a decimal number

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:02.320
Which is 0.8633

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:06.040
Now one rule with using this table is that

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:12.280
if there were two significant figures in the number we were taking the log of,

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:17.720
then we do 2 minus 1 and we put a 1 before our decimal point.

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:20.260
It's always going to be n minus 1, I believe.

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:25.320
So it's going to be 1 point, and then our number, 8 6 3 3

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:32.400
Add that to the log of 19. Let's go and find 1.9 which will be here,

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:38.120
that corresponds to 0.2788

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:46.980
So in a similar way that will be 1 (two significant figures) point 2788

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:58.220
If we add those together what we get is 3.1421

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:01.540
That was suspiciously close to pi there for a second.

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:03.020
OK.

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:09.860
That means that if we take the antilog of this number here we should end up with the product that we wanted.

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:14.660
So we'd get another table which is our anti-log table,

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:21.980
you better hope you've got all these handy, and we're going to look up 3.1421

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:26.520
But we're actually just going to look up the part that's after the decimal point in here.

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:29.680
So 0.1421

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:41.220
0.142 and then we're going to add on a 1 which is adding on a 0,

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:47.160
so it's just going to be 0.1387

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:47.900
OK.

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:55.140
So what we're going to get back up here as our answer for the product 73 * 19

00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:58.920
will be this value. It will be

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:04.220
0.1387

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:06.400
and then we have a rule that works in reverse,

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:10.660
so remember this 3, you add 1 on to that, becomes 4,

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:15.360
and we times our answer by 10 to the power of 4.

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:25.180
That works out our answer to just be 1,387 as the product between those two numbers.

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:29.320
Now remember what we were actually doing was this up here.

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:37.580
So this is actually equal to 1,387 over

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:43.680
1000 times a 100 will be 1 (counting zeros) one, two, three, four, five...

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:47.880
100,000 and that is our answer (1,387/100,000).

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:58.220
If we look at question 3 now, we're being asked to divide 2880 by 0.0036

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:01.900
Now to do division on our slide rule,

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:06.660
we just do the opposite of what we were doing when we were doing multiplication

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:10.300
So what we want to do is line up

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:16.020
3.6 on our C slider on top of

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:23.060
2.88 on our bottom line, which is our D and we'll see what we get there

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:36.340
So we've got 3.6 lining up with 2.88 roughly there.

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:41.620
And then what we want to do is trace it along and

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:44.600
figure out what our 1 lines up with.

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:47.180
Now, the 1 on the side is off the page. So that's not helpful.

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:50.200
We're going to go to this 1 over here,

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:55.320
and we can see that lines up almost perfectly with our 8.

00:13:55.520 --> 00:14:00.560
So our answer to this division question,

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:12.340
Is going to be 8 something, and you maybe need a bit of guess and check on this...

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:20.600
I think that we're going to have 8 (counting zeros) one, two, three, four, five...

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:25.740
...that feels kind of right but I'm gonna go check on my calculator and see.

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:33.360
Yes, verifying on my calculator which is not what you'd be allowed to do in this exam,

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:38.020
looks like this is our right answer here, 800,000. So there you go.

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:43.680
There's three different types of questions solved using either a slide rule or a log table.

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:49.240
We've seen division, multiplication, and multiplication with a bit more accuracy.

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:54.920
The rest of the questions here on this exam I am going to leave as an exercise for the viewer.

